 "methodology": [
  "<b>One observation = one position</b> (wallet \u00d7 market), never a single trade fill. 118 fills of one bet is one decision, not 118 wins.",
  "<b>ROI = \u03a3 P&amp;L / \u03a3 capital</b>, capital-weighted. Never an average of percentages \u2014 averaging returns lets a $2 bet outweigh a $20,000 one.",
  "<b>Z</b> = (win rate \u2212 average price) / \u221a(p(1\u2212p)/n). A wallet buying at 80\u00a2 needs an 80% win rate just to break even; Z measures the gap against that baseline.",
  "<b>ROI lower bound</b> = 5th percentile of 1,000 bootstrap resamples of the wallet's own positions. A conservative estimate: what it would still show in a bad scenario.",
  "The main ranking sorts by <b>ROI lower bound, not ROI</b> \u2014 this rewards evidence rather than luck.",
  "Minimum <b>8 resolved positions</b> to enter the main ranking. Smaller samples are listed below with a label, not hidden.",
  "All resolutions come from <b>official Polymarket settlements</b> (Gamma API), not from price proxies \u2014 a proxy can disagree with the real outcome by 12%.",
  "<b>Entries priced above 90\u00a2 are excluded from edge metrics</b> and counted separately. Buying at 98.7\u00a2 risks $86k to make $3k \u2014 that is scalping pennies, not finding edge, and it inflates win rates (one loss erases 99 wins). Those positions are reported in the \"scalps\" column instead of being hidden.",
  "<b>We publish every tracked wallet</b>, including the losing ones. Removing them would turn this into advertising.",
  "<b>No signals for sale, no fees, no positions of our own</b>, no affiliation with Polymarket."
